# Architecture

Use the existing Next.js App Router structure.

- `app/` owns routes and route-level composition.
- `components/` owns interactive UI and shared presentation.
- `components/ui/` contains UI primitives; compose them instead of modifying them casually.
- `lib/projects.ts`, `lib/departments.ts`, and `lib/hardware.ts` contain seeded catalog data.
- `lib/types.ts` defines the shared domain model.
- `components/operator-provider.tsx` owns current device-local operator state.

## State boundaries

- Static project facts belong in `lib/` catalog files.
- User-created captures, field logs, evidence, hardware overrides, pins, publish state, and checkpoints belong in operator state.
- Do not duplicate authoritative state across components.
- Derive filtered lists, counts, and merged hardware views at read time.
- Keep browser-only APIs inside client components.

## Integration boundaries

- Keep GitHub, Alfred, Whisper, Tailscale, telemetry, and recording transport behind explicit adapters or API clients when implemented.
- Do not call external services directly from presentation components.
- Do not add credentials, host addresses, tokens, or private family data to source control.
- Alfred suggestions must enter The Drop as reviewable proposals. Do not auto-file, overwrite, publish, or delete operator records without an explicit approval step.

Prefer extending the existing architecture over introducing a new state framework, database, or service abstraction. Add infrastructure only when the requested workflow requires it.
